    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nHonda Auto Stop-Start Issue Prompts NHTSA Investigation, 1 Million Vehicles Potentially Affected\n23 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nThe safety boffins at the Office of Safety Investigations have opened an engineering analysis into American Honda Motor vehicles with automatic start-stop systems that may not work as intended. More specifically, these systems may fail to restart the engine automatically from a complete stop.\nSaid investigation covers a whopping 1,033,632 automobiles produced for model years 2024 through 2026. Meanwhile, the latest American Honda Motor service documentation instructs owners to disable the system while the manufacturer deals with limited replacement parts availability.\nEA26007 covers the Odyssey minivan, Passport and Pilot crossovers, and the Ridgeline pickup, together with the Pilot-twinned Acura MDX. The federal agency has collected 701 complaints as of this writing, alleging that the engine doesn't restart automatically after the driver releases the brake pedal. Keeping the engine off when the traffic light turns green or when the driver wants to make a turn at an intersection definitely sounds like a safety risk.\nPer service bulletin 26-090, vehicles may also experience a slow crank or fail to start under hot ambient temperature conditions. Published by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration on October 30, 2026, the bulletin states that repairs are available only for vehicles experiencing symptoms. American Honda Motor blames limited parts availability.\n1-888-234-2138 is the customer support and campaign center number customers can call for additional information. The bulletin covers 2020 through 2026 Odyssey models, the 2018 to 2019 Odyssey Touring and Elite, 2019 through 2025 Passport models, the 2016 to 2022 Pilot, and the 2020 to 2026 Ridgeline. Customers are further advised to check their VINs to establish eligibility.\nPhoto: Honda Acura doesn't appear in the cited service bulletin, and the bulletin does not explain which parts are being replaced. Once sufficient replacement parts become available, Honda plans another notification. More curious still, the saga kicked off years ago, back in June 2022. It all started with PE22005, a preliminary evaluation prompted by complaints about the Auto Idle Stop system in older Pilots.\nThe NHTSA's ODI subsequently expanded the examination, elevating it to an engineering analysis in March 2025. EA25004 covered a simply ludicrous 2,209,466 vehicles, including the TLX sedan. Its opening report highlighted 1,348 complaints, four crash incidents, and two injuries, and the NHTSA also learned about owners who needed a jump-start before they could continue driving.\nCountermeasures were introduced in January 2023 through service bulletins 23-008 and 23-009. If updating the fuel injection software failed to fix things, authorized dealers were required to replace the starter assembly, relay, and adjust the valves. In addition, American Honda Motor extended warranty coverage to 10 years with unlimited mileage for vehicles that needed replacement parts.\nThe complaints, however, continued after these corrective actions. Per the NHTSA, many owners claim that dealers had already completed the prescribed countermeasures on their vehicles. Adding to the confusion, EA26007 reads that vehicles involved in the current investigation appear to have different root causes for their auto start-stop woes. They also appear to respond differently to repairs.\nBeing an engineering analysis, EA26007 aims to establish the potential safety risks, severity, and scope of the problem. At the same time, Honda Motor America needs to establish why newer vehicles apparently require a different approach from those involved in the earlier investigation.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda Acura investigation safety NHTSA\nDownload: Honda service bulletin 23-009 from January 2023 (PDF)\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT China's Most Distant Space Mission Is Going to Jupiter, Has Room on Board for More Science Payloads",
